              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.301,
            0.37,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Find x in Fig. 5.26."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.745,
            0.322,
            0.822,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 5.26"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.338,
            0.492,
            0.36
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.8 Concyclicity of Points"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.367,
            0.885,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now we address the question about when 4 points lie on the same circle. And we will let you explore the question for 5 points, 6 points and so on! We call points that are on the same circle "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "concyclic",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.429,
            0.886,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Theorem 10:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If a line segment AB joining two points A, B subtends equal angles at two other points C, D that lie on the same side of AB, then the four points lie on a circle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.491,
            0.543,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As always we draw the picture first."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.514,
            0.886,
            0.554
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Given:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Consider segment AB. Points C and D lie on the same side of AB; points C and D are not on the line AB; and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.557,
            0.599,
            0.577
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To show:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " A, B, C, D lie on the same circle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.581,
            0.883,
            0.622
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why is this true?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The points A, B, C are noncollinear points. So, using Theorem 1, there is a circle passing through A, B, C."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.625,
            0.884,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us show that D also lies on that circle. For this, let us draw the circle through A, B and C."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.667,
            0.884,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Suppose D is not on the circle; then it is either inside or outside the circle. Join AD."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.711,
